---
layout: layouts/base.njk
---
{#
  Article layout: the existing frame, plus a title, a byline and the body.
  Nothing else. This is step one of the article system and deliberately adds no
  chrome of its own.

  It chains to layouts/base.njk rather than restating the shell, which is how
  the five marketing pages already get the masthead, the colophon and the
  JSON-LD block. Nothing here rebuilds any of that.

  MEASURE. base/elements.css already caps p at 66ch, so paragraphs were at
  measure before this layout existed. Headings, lists, blockquotes and
  everything else were NOT: without a wrapper they would run the full 1120px of
  .cs-container while the paragraphs between them sat at 66ch, which reads as a
  broken column rather than a narrow one. So the cap belongs on the wrapper.

  max-w-measure is the utility --container-measure already generates, so this
  introduces no CSS. It also makes p's own 66ch redundant here, harmlessly: the
  two are the same value, and elements.css still needs its rule for prose that
  is not inside this layout.

  The <article> element is deliberate rather than reusing the section() macro,
  which emits <section>. Semantics are the point on a page that is one article.
#}
{#- The `header` field picks the title treatment (SHORTCODES.md: standard,
    feature, compact, cover, glyph). standard and feature share the base scale,
    so only compact and cover have rules; an unset field falls through to the
    base, which is what a piece with no opinion should get. -#}
<article class="cs-article{{ " cs-article--" + header if header }}">
  <div class="cs-article__column cs-prose">
  {#- cover is the one header treatment that is a component rather than a
      scale. It replaces the standard header outright, carrying the kind, the
      title, the deck and the byline fields in its own panel, so nothing below
      is rendered twice. See partials/cover.njk. -#}
  {%- if header == "cover" %}
  {% include "partials/cover.njk" %}
  {%- else %}
  <header>
    <p class="caption">{{ kind }}</p>

    <h1>{{ title }}</h1>

    {% if deck %}<p class="cs-article__deck">{{ deck }}</p>{% endif %}

    {# author is a KEY per SHORTCODES.md, never a display name, and resolves
       against _data/authors.js. An unknown key falls back to the key itself
       rather than to nothing: a lowercase handle sitting in a byline is
       obviously wrong and greppable, whereas a blank byline reads as an
       article nobody wrote and could ship unnoticed. #}
    {%- set byline = authors[author] -%}
    {# The date is emitted twice on purpose: the visible string for a reader,
       and the machine date in datetime= for an answer engine deciding whether
       this piece is current. Both filters read UTC parts, so the two can never
       disagree by a day. #}
    <p class="caption">{{ byline.name if byline else author }}, updated
      <time datetime="{{ updated | isoDate }}">{{ updated | calendarDate }}</time></p>
  </header>
  {%- endif %}

  {{ content | safe }}
  </div>
</article>

{#- THE AUDIT STRIP. A worklist that travels with the page, on the preview host
    and never on production.

    GATED ON deploy.indexable, WHICH IS THE SAME CONDITION THE NOINDEX USES.
    Not a second switch and not an environment variable of its own: there is
    exactly one place in this repo that decides which deploy this is, and it
    already throws rather than guessing. Reusing it means the strip cannot end
    up on the live site while the robots tag says preview, or the reverse. See
    src/_data/deploy.js.

    Local builds are not indexable either, so the strip is on in development,
    which is where it is most useful.

    A SIBLING OF THE ARTICLE, NOT ITS LAST CHILD. Two reasons, and either alone
    would settle it. It is not article content: it is scaffolding about the
    article, which is what an aside outside the article says. And the seam rule
    in article.css matches `.cs-article:has(> .cs-cta:last-child)`, so a strip
    inside would displace a trailing cta from :last-child and reopen the gap
    that rule was written to close, on preview only, which is exactly the kind
    of difference nobody would think to look for.

    THREE OF THE FOUR SECTIONS ARE FILLED BY A TRANSFORM, because a placeholder
    slot, a bracketed value and an observed marker only exist once the page is
    rendered. The frontmatter section is here because it is the one the built
    page cannot answer: a missing field leaves no trace to find. See the
    auditStrip transform in eleventy.config.js. -#}
{%- if not deploy.indexable %}
<aside class="cs-audit" aria-label="Preview audit strip">
  {#- data-audit is on the INNER element, because that is where the transform
      appends. Appending before the aside's own close would drop three sections
      outside the wrapper that gives the strip its width. -#}
  <div class="cs-audit__inner" data-audit>
    <p class="cs-audit__title">Audit strip</p>
    <p class="cs-audit__note">Preview and local builds only. Never rendered on production. What this page still owes.</p>

    <section class="cs-audit__section">
      <h2 class="cs-audit__heading">Frontmatter</h2>
      {%- if auditFrontmatter.length %}
      <ul class="cs-audit__list">
        {%- for item in auditFrontmatter %}
        <li><span class="cs-audit__key">{{ item.field }}</span> <span class="cs-audit__state">{{ item.state }}</span>
          {%- if item.value %} <span class="cs-audit__value">{{ item.value }}</span>{% endif %}
          <span class="cs-audit__why">{{ item.why }}</span></li>
        {%- endfor %}
      </ul>
      {%- else %}
      <p class="cs-audit__clear">Nothing outstanding.</p>
      {%- endif %}
    </section>
  </div>
</aside>
{%- endif %}
